{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEpic fattish-plucky-bufflehead (62dc-535b-5d05-421d) needs a retrieval architecture chosen on measurement, not the July N = 8 experiment. The store holds a labeled set — every recorded `duplicates` link, closed transitively into clusters — but it must be re-derived, since eac3's numbers are a July snapshot.\n\nRe-measured on tickets-branch 0b9294311b5 (5,663 live tickets): 482 `duplicates` links close into 148 clusters over 630 tickets, giving **1,524 within-cluster pairs** (eac3: 51). But **1,431 of them (94%) have byte-identical normalized titles** — 84 single-title clusters that are bulk-creation artifacts, not authored duplicates. Pooling them would score ~100% recall for the cheapest lexical candidate and reject vector search on an artifact, inverting the epic's decision.\n\n**The 93 differing-title pairs are the semantic set and the benchmark** — double eac3's 44, same kind, from 64 mixed clusters. The parent epic carries the full rationale.\n\n## Approach\n\nBuild the fixture at a pinned tickets-branch SHA: collect every recorded `duplicates` link (`rebar list --full --exclude-deleted -o json`, reading each ticket's `deps`), close transitively into clusters, enumerate within-cluster pairs, and **stratify each pair `lexical` (normalized titles equal, case/punctuation/whitespace folded) or `semantic` (titles differ)**. Store per ticket, in `evals/similar-tickets/benchmark.json`: pair ids, aliases, stratum, title, and the **`digest_input`** — title, full description, then every comment oldest-first, each separated by a blank line, truncated to 40,000 characters — with the SHA and per-stratum counts in a header object.\n\nReport recall@5 and recall@10 **per stratum, never pooled** — the semantic stratum is the decision criterion, the lexical stratum a control every candidate should near-saturate. Query text is title plus the first 200 characters of description (the compose-time shape). Candidates:\n\n1. Lexical baseline: SQLite FTS5 with `bm25()` over title (weight 5.0) and description (weight 1.0) in a temporary index built from the fixture.\n2. Digest lexical: BM25F over LLM digests produced with the retired `ticket_digest` prompt and its `TicketDigest` contract, read from commit **f147e08242f**, the parent of 2de5f341ec4 (\"Remove the overlap/enrichment implementation\"): prompt text `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/ticket_digest.md`, contract `src/rebar/llm/contracts.py`. **Only the prompt text and output shape are reused — the deleted `overlap/` orchestration is NOT restored**; the eval calls the model directly through the `rebar.llm` runner. Model: the **`trivial`** class (`src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py`, default `claude-haiku-4-5`, override `REBAR_LLM_TRIVIAL_MODEL`), resolved id in the fixture header. One call per ticket, **fed exactly the fixture's `digest_input`** — the title + description + every comment the original prompt consumed (epic 62dc's architecture comment), NOT a truncated description. The returned `TicketDigest` JSON is **committed to `evals/similar-tickets/digests.json`**, keyed by ticket id plus a SHA-256 of `digest_input` and the prompt text; the runner reuses a cached digest on a key match and calls the model only on a miss, so the recall tables are recomputable from the committed fixture with no model access.\n3. Vector: embeddings of an LLM summary (the same digest text joined as one paragraph) from Amazon Titan Text Embeddings V2, model id `amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0`, via boto3 `bedrock-runtime` `invoke_model` with body `{\"inputText\": ..., \"dimensions\": 1024, \"normalize\": true}`, region from `rebar.toml` `[llm] bedrock_region_name` (`us-east-1`, rebar.toml:213). Cosine similarity by brute force; 630 normalized vectors need no ANN library. Call mechanics and limits: see comment.\n\n**Auth precondition — the Titan call is NOT entitled today.** `infra/terraform/iam_s7.tf` (sid `ConverseAnthropicClaudeOnly`) scopes `bedrock:InvokeModel` to `anthropic.claude-*` ARNs only; no Titan or embedding ARN exists in `infra/`, so `amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0` returns `AccessDeniedException` there (the shape ADR 0059:307 records). Candidate 2's `claude-haiku-4-5` is entitled; candidate 3 is not. Resolution: run candidate 3 under a developer AWS identity holding Titan entitlement (a one-off offline eval, not a production path) and record that identity and its entitlement check in the fixture header; failing that, add the Titan ARN to `iam_s7.tf` first. The credentials-absent skip is **widened to catch `AccessDeniedException`**, recording candidate 3 `unavailable` with the error instead of aborting — and an `unavailable` candidate may not be selected by the ADR.\n\n**Digest serialization (candidates 2 and 3).** `TicketDigest` is a 4-field structured contract — `problem_keywords`, `component_or_area`, `key_entities`, `propositions` (all `list[str]` but `component_or_area: str`) — not free text, so its mapping to a retrievable document is fixed here rather than left to the executor. Candidate 2 indexes the **four as BM25F fields**, weights `propositions 3.0`, `problem_keywords 2.0`, `key_entities 2.0`, `component_or_area 1.0`; lists joined with `\" \"`. Candidate 3 embeds the **same four in the same order** as one paragraph, each on one line prefixed by its field name, truncated to 40,000 characters. The weights are the one free parameter, recorded in the ADR and not tuned per candidate.\n\nWrite the ADR (number = one above the highest `docs/adr/.numbers/` marker, written by `gen_adr_index.py`; no script computes a next free number) recording the per-stratum recall tables, the chosen architecture, the bulk-duplicate artifact and why the strata are reported separately, the index location (`<repo>/.git/rebar/similar.sqlite`, keyed by tickets-branch HEAD SHA plus a format version, rebuilt on mismatch), the catch-up rule (reader-process incremental update from `git diff --name-only <indexed_sha> <head>`), and the cost model (summarization and embedding calls per changed ticket).\n\n## Scope\n\n- `evals/similar-tickets/{benchmark.json,digests.json,measure.py,README.md}` — all new; `measure.py` runs the three candidates. The top-level `evals/` tree already exists (commit 91d39fd6061), so the eval-harness dependency this story once waited on has landed.\n- `docs/adr/0NNN-similar-ticket-retrieval.md` (new).\n- `docs/adr/README.md` (regenerated by `scripts/gen_adr_index.py`).\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: `python evals/similar-tickets/measure.py --candidate fts5` prints a per-stratum recall table; the fixture loads and every pair resolves to two existing ticket ids.\n- Edge: a pair whose ticket has an empty description still yields a query from the title alone; the runner skips a candidate cleanly when Bedrock credentials are absent and says so.\n- Edge: `--check` fails loudly if the fixture's recorded tickets-branch SHA is not an ancestor of the current tickets HEAD, so a stale fixture cannot be silently measured.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `evals/similar-tickets/benchmark.json` records the tickets-branch SHA it was derived at, and every pair carries ids, aliases, titles, description heads and a `lexical`/`semantic` stratum. `measure.py --check` resolves every id in the store and re-derives the pair set at that SHA, matching the fixture exactly. Checked by running the command.\n- [x] Recall@5 and recall@10 are recorded **per stratum** for all three candidates in the ADR, produced by `measure.py` with the exact command lines quoted. No pooled figure is reported. Checked by re-running each command and matching the ADR tables.\n- [x] The ADR names one architecture and justifies it from the **semantic** stratum, and records the index path, freshness key, catch-up rule, embedding model, per-ticket cost, and the bulk-duplicate artifact that makes stratification necessary. Checked by reading the ADR against `measure.py` output.\n- [x] `scripts/gen_adr_index.py --check` and `scripts/check_adr_numbers.py` both exit 0."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793521903480549001,"uuid":"477b3757-4d1a-48d5-9189-895432a93c85"}